# Intuition

To find the intersection of two arrays while considering duplicate elements, we need to include an element as many times as it appears in both arrays.

For example, if `2` appears twice in `nums1` and three times in `nums2`, the result should contain `2` twice.

By sorting both arrays, equal elements are placed next to each other. We can then use two pointers to compare the elements of both arrays and find their intersection efficiently.

# Approach

1. Sort both arrays.
2. Use two pointers:
   - `i` points to `nums1`.
   - `j` points to `nums2`.
3. Compare `nums1[i]` and `nums2[j]`:
   - If `nums1[i] < nums2[j]`, increment `i`.
   - If `nums2[j] < nums1[i]`, increment `j`.
   - If both are equal, add the element to the result and increment both pointers.
4. Continue until either pointer reaches the end of its array.
5. Convert the `ArrayList` into an integer array and return it.

Since duplicates are allowed, we add every matching element without checking whether it was already added.

# Complexity

- Time complexity: O(n log n + m log m)
- Space complexity: O(n + m)

# Code

```java
class Solution {

    public int[] intersect(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {

        int n = nums1.length;
        int m = nums2.length;

        Arrays.sort(nums1);
        Arrays.sort(nums2);

        ArrayList<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        int i = 0, j = 0;

        while (i < n && j < m) {

            if (nums1[i] < nums2[j]) {
                i++;
            }
            else if (nums2[j] < nums1[i]) {
                j++;
            }
            else {
                ans.add(nums1[i]);
                i++;
                j++;
            }
        }

        int a[] = new int[ans.size()];

        for (int k = 0; k < ans.size(); k++) {
            a[k] = ans.get(k);
        }

        return a;
    }
}
